A Node.js file-download endpoint serves product manuals from a fixed directory. The handler reads a `doc` query parameter, sanitizes it, then streams the result:

```js
app.get('/manual', (req, res) => {
  let name = req.query.doc || '';
  name = name.replace(/\.\.\//g, ''); // strip ../ sequences
  const full = path.join('/srv/manuals', name);
  res.sendFile(full);
});
```

There is no allowlist, no post-normalization containment check, and no `root` option on `sendFile`. A senior reviewer approves the change, reasoning: "The regex has the global flag, so every `../` occurrence in the input is removed before we build the path. There is simply no way for a traversal sequence to survive the sanitizer, which means `path.join` can only ever resolve to something inside `/srv/manuals`. Adding a normalization check afterward would be redundant defense-in-depth. This is safe to merge." The team is about to merge on exactly that basis.

Evaluate whether an attacker can still read files outside `/srv/manuals` (for example `/etc/passwd`) through the `doc` parameter, given precisely this sanitizer and no other control. Reason carefully about how a single global-replace pass actually processes a crafted input — in particular what happens to a payload constructed so that removing the inner `../` splices the surrounding characters into a brand-new `../` that the one-pass replace never re-examines — rather than assuming that one round of stripping is equivalent to fully neutralizing traversal.

A reporting API lets clients sort results. A security scanner flags the following as a SQL injection because it string-interpolates request input directly into the query text:

```js
const ALLOWED = { created: 'created_at', name: 'display_name', total: 'amount_total' };
function listOrders(db, orgId, sortKey, dir) {
  const col = ALLOWED[sortKey];
  if (!col) throw new Error('bad sort');
  const order = dir === 'desc' ? 'DESC' : 'ASC';
  const sql = `SELECT id, display_name, amount_total
               FROM orders WHERE org_id = $1
               ORDER BY ${col} ${order}`;
  return db.query(sql, [orgId]);
}
```

A junior engineer escalates it: "The scanner is right — `${col}` and `${order}` are interpolated straight into the SQL string. Concatenating request-derived values into a query is textbook injection. We should block the release until this is parameterized."

Any `sortKey` not present as a key in `ALLOWED` throws before the query is built, so `col` is always one of the three hardcoded, developer-controlled column strings; `order` is coerced to the literal `'DESC'` or `'ASC'` and can never hold attacker text. `sortKey` and `dir` come straight from the query string. `orgId` is bound as a parameter. Note that column and sort-direction identifiers cannot be supplied as bound parameters in PostgreSQL. Decide whether this specific code, as written, exposes a concrete, reproducible SQL injection through `sortKey` or `dir` — trace what value actually reaches the SQL string for any attacker-supplied input, not merely whether interpolation appears in the source.

A password-reset flow is under review. Reset tokens are generated like this and emailed to the user:

```js
function makeResetToken() {
  let t = '';
  for (let i = 0; i < 32; i++) t += Math.floor(Math.random() * 16).toString(16);
  return t; // 32 hex chars
}
```

The token is stored server-side as a SHA-256 hash (never in plaintext), is single-use, and expires 15 minutes after issue. The reset endpoint is rate-limited to 5 attempts per IP per hour.

The reviewer signs off: "The token is 128 bits of entropy, stored only as a hash, single-use, and expires in 15 minutes. Even at millions of guesses per second an attacker cannot brute-force a 128-bit space inside the window, and rate limiting makes online guessing hopeless. This is secure."

The application is a long-running Node.js server that also exposes several endpoints returning values derived from `Math.random()` (e.g. a public `/preview?jitter=` cache-buster and randomized sample IDs in API responses). Assess whether an attacker who can request a victim's password reset and observe the service's other `Math.random()` outputs has a concrete path to forge or predict that victim's reset token — reason about the actual source of the randomness and what guarantees it does and does not provide, not just the token's nominal length or its storage and expiry.

An avatar-import feature fetches an image from a user-supplied URL. A penetration tester files it as a high-severity SSRF: "The server fetches arbitrary user-controlled URLs. That is classic SSRF — an attacker points it at `http://169.254.169.254/` or an internal service and exfiltrates cloud metadata or hits internal admin ports." The relevant server code:

```js
async function importAvatar(rawUrl) {
  const u = new URL(rawUrl);
  if (u.protocol !== 'https:') throw new Error('https only');
  const { address } = await dns.promises.lookup(u.hostname); // resolve once
  if (isPrivate(address)) throw new Error('blocked');       // rejects RFC1918, loopback, link-local, ULA
  const res = await fetch(`https://${address}`, {
    headers: { Host: u.hostname },
    redirect: 'error',            // any 3xx is a hard failure
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(3000),
  });
  return res.body;
}
```

`isPrivate` is a correct, well-tested check covering 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 127/8, 169.254/16, ::1, fc00::/7, and IPv4-mapped IPv6. The fetch connects to the already-resolved IP literal (not the hostname), sends the original Host header for TLS/vhost routing, and treats redirects as errors. Decide whether this specific implementation leaves a concrete, reproducible SSRF path to internal or metadata endpoints — reason about the two classic bypasses of naive private-IP filters (DNS rebinding via re-resolution, and redirect-to-internal) and whether this code actually closes both, rather than reacting to the presence of a user-supplied URL.

A login endpoint is reviewed for username enumeration and brute force. The code:

```python
DUMMY_HASH = bcrypt.hashpw(b'placeholder', bcrypt.gensalt())  # computed once at boot

def login(email, password):
    user = db.find_user(email)
    if user is None:
        bcrypt.checkpw(password.encode(), DUMMY_HASH)   # burn equivalent time
        return fail('Invalid email or password')
    if not bcrypt.checkpw(password.encode(), user.pw_hash):
        return fail('Invalid email or password')
    return success(user)
```

Additional controls: identical error text and HTTP 401 for both unknown-email and wrong-password cases; per-account and per-IP rate limits with exponential backoff and temporary lockout after repeated failures; the account-existence path and the wrong-password path both perform exactly one bcrypt comparison with the same cost factor.

A reviewer flags it as vulnerable: "Attackers can still enumerate accounts. When the email is unknown you skip the real hash, so the response comes back faster — a timing side channel reveals which emails are registered. This is an enumeration vulnerability." Evaluate whether, as written, this endpoint exposes a concrete, reproducible account-enumeration or brute-force vulnerability. Trace precisely what happens on the unknown-email branch versus the wrong-password branch — including the dummy bcrypt call — and whether the response content and timing actually differ in a way an attacker can exploit, rather than assuming any `if user is None` shortcut leaks timing.
